<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="55709" href="schedule/2015/fall/PSYC/593/55709.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2015" href="schedule/2015.xml">2015</calendarYear><term id="120158" href="schedule/2015/fall.xml">Fall 2015</term><subject id="PSYC" href="schedule/2015/fall/PSYC.xml">Psychology</subject><course id="593" href="schedule/2015/fall/PSYC/593.xml">Seminar</course></parents><sectionNumber>ML </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Restorative Justice</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This is a seminar course investigating the theory, principles, and practices of restorative justice, with an emphasis on Restorative Circles (RC), a restorative practice originally developed in Brazil.  The course will have a strong applied component.  Students are encouraged to practice/apply the work within their own social networks. Some students may be invited to observe and possibly even lead Circles in the community.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2015-08-24Z</startDate><endDate>2015-12-09Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>09:00 AM</start><end>10:50 AM</end><daysOfTheWeek>W      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>708</roomNumber><buildingName>Psychology Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Lyubansky" firstName="M">Lyubansky, M</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>